# This file is read LIVE by the running sshd, which is started with
#   sshd.exe -f <this path> -D -E %USERPROFILE%\.portable-sshd.log
# so it is the running config, not a template that gets copied somewhere. A bad
# edit here takes effect the next time sshd restarts. Validate before restarting:
#   sshd.exe -t -f <this path>
#
# Only AuthorizedKeysFile expands tokens (%h = the user's home). HostKey,
# Subsystem and ForceCommand are literal paths with no variable expansion, so
# they cannot be de-pinned from the user name -- do not try.
Port 2222
HostKey C:/Users/jason.christiansen/GitHub/dotfiles/application_configs/portable_sshd/ssh_host_rsa_key
PubkeyAuthentication yes
PasswordAuthentication yes
AuthorizedKeysFile %h/GitHub/dotfiles/application_configs/portable_sshd/authorized_keys
Subsystem sftp C:/Users/jason.christiansen/userapps/OpenSSH-Win64/sftp-server.exe
# No admin means no HKLM DefaultShell, so sshd would drop interactive logins
# into cmd.exe. The wrapper starts PowerShell for interactive sessions and
# passes SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND (exec/scp/rsync/sftp requests) through cmd /c
# unchanged. The command MUST start with cmd.exe: pty sessions CreateProcess
# the forced command directly (no shell), which cannot execute a bare .cmd
# file. Restart sshd after changing this.
ForceCommand cmd.exe /c C:\Users\jason.christiansen\GitHub\dotfiles\application_configs\portable_sshd\shell_wrapper.cmd